WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.740
So I want you to think about the last time you did something really complicated at work, like highly complex.

00:07.980 --> 00:08.400
Oh, boy.

00:08.740 --> 00:09.220
Yeah, right.

00:09.220 --> 00:17.240
Like maybe you ran a convoluted software diagnostic or you had to follow some dense, you know, multi-step medical protocol.

00:17.460 --> 00:22.240
And if someone just stopped you right in the middle of it and asked, why are you doing it exactly that way?

00:22.940 --> 00:24.760
Like, what would your honest answer be?

00:25.160 --> 00:29.360
I mean, for most people, it's definitely not that they tested every alternative.

00:30.000 --> 00:30.080
Right.

00:30.220 --> 00:33.900
You wouldn't say, oh, I mapped out every possible path and found the optimal one.

00:33.940 --> 00:37.000
You'd probably say, well, because that's what the manual says.

00:37.200 --> 00:40.760
Or, you know, because the senior guy on my team told me to do it this way.

00:40.960 --> 00:41.360
Exactly.

00:41.760 --> 00:44.580
And, you know, for almost all of us, we don't usually question it, right?

00:44.600 --> 00:45.860
Because the outcome is successful.

00:46.340 --> 00:46.540
Yeah.

00:46.640 --> 00:50.080
We get the machine working, the patient stabilizes, the code compiles.

00:50.620 --> 00:52.500
So we feel like experts.

00:52.500 --> 00:54.240
We feel like we know what we're doing.

00:54.240 --> 01:05.060
But today, we're going to look at why that feeling of expertise might actually be a massive, invisible vulnerability.

01:05.520 --> 01:06.140
It really is.

01:06.400 --> 01:12.420
We are diving into this fascinating 2026 independent research paper by Flyxion.

01:12.680 --> 01:17.660
It's titled, Borrowed Intuition, Compression Without Experience.

01:17.820 --> 01:18.740
Such a great title.

01:18.740 --> 01:19.520
It is.

01:19.880 --> 01:32.420
And the mission of this deep dive today is to really deconstruct the sheer, I mean, staggering gap between performing an expert action and actually generating the knowledge behind it.

01:32.420 --> 01:32.660
Right.

01:32.660 --> 01:41.540
We're going to look at how humans, massive institutions, and yes, even, you know, the most advanced AI models are basically entirely dependent on these brilliant shortcuts.

01:41.960 --> 01:42.180
Yeah.

01:42.280 --> 01:46.220
It's a framework that completely reshapes how you view your own competence, really.

01:46.320 --> 01:53.300
Because the paper argues that just having fluency in a task, that doesn't actually guarantee you understand the system you're manipulating.

01:53.300 --> 01:58.560
It just proves you've inherited a really good shortcut from someone else's painful trial and error.

01:58.580 --> 01:59.080
From their pain.

01:59.300 --> 01:59.460
Yeah.

01:59.460 --> 01:59.900
Exactly.

02:00.400 --> 02:08.180
And when the environment shifts, that hidden difference between understanding and just performing becomes a matter of, well, success or catastrophic failure.

02:08.400 --> 02:08.600
Right.

02:08.640 --> 02:14.980
So to really wrap our heads around the scale of this, like how much we rely on these shortcuts, I want to start exactly where the paper starts.

02:15.120 --> 02:16.620
Getting away from the tech for a minute.

02:16.620 --> 02:17.020
Yeah.

02:17.140 --> 02:17.960
Away from algorithms.

02:18.240 --> 02:19.480
Away from the corporate bureaucracy.

02:19.880 --> 02:21.920
Just down at the most basic human level.

02:22.360 --> 02:23.780
The apprentice and the master.

02:24.060 --> 02:24.960
I love this example.

02:24.960 --> 02:31.860
It's the perfect starting point to, you know, isolate the exact mechanism of how learning works.

02:32.040 --> 02:32.740
Lay it out for us.

02:33.040 --> 02:36.540
So the author sets up this scenario in a mechanic's garage.

02:36.780 --> 02:42.120
You have an apprentice who is just staring down a really stubborn fault in an engine.

02:42.360 --> 02:42.660
Okay.

02:42.920 --> 02:50.040
And all the obvious visible symptoms point to this one specific, easily accessible component being damaged.

02:50.040 --> 02:54.740
So the apprentice, you know, grabs their tools, ready to tear the whole assembly apart.

02:55.080 --> 02:55.660
Like you do.

02:55.960 --> 02:56.220
Right.

02:56.600 --> 03:03.380
But the master mechanic walks by, takes one look, and says, stop, check the hidden wiring connection tucked behind the firewall first.

03:03.620 --> 03:03.940
Okay.

03:04.020 --> 03:05.220
So he just gives them the answer.

03:05.560 --> 03:05.940
Basically.

03:06.440 --> 03:13.720
The apprentice trusts the master, grabs a flashlight, checks that hidden connection, and sure enough, there's the defect.

03:14.380 --> 03:17.580
Problem is solved in five minutes instead of five hours.

03:17.800 --> 03:18.560
Which is awesome.

03:18.560 --> 03:19.140
It is.

03:19.800 --> 03:21.800
But here is where it gets interesting.

03:22.640 --> 03:23.880
Fast forward six months.

03:24.680 --> 03:28.640
A different car rolls into the shop with the exact same symptoms.

03:28.900 --> 03:28.980
Right.

03:29.220 --> 03:31.340
The apprentice doesn't even pause.

03:31.640 --> 03:35.960
They walk straight up, reach behind the firewall, and check that hidden connection.

03:36.440 --> 03:40.020
And to anyone watching, the apprentice looks like a seasoned genius.

03:40.220 --> 03:40.540
Right.

03:40.640 --> 03:42.080
Because they just did it instantly.

03:42.080 --> 03:49.000
But if we pause there and look at the actual cognitive reality of what just happened, it's kind of an illusion.

03:49.160 --> 03:50.100
It's completely an illusion.

03:50.340 --> 03:54.700
Because if you walked into the shop on that second day, you'd see this rapid, flawless judgment.

03:54.700 --> 04:02.940
You'd assume, oh, this kid has spent years making mistakes on this engine, testing all these different hypotheses, and finally internalizing the pattern.

04:03.020 --> 04:03.240
Right.

04:03.380 --> 04:04.660
But they didn't do any of that.

04:04.720 --> 04:05.440
The master did.

04:05.440 --> 04:05.960
Exactly.

04:06.100 --> 04:07.100
The master paid the price.

04:07.220 --> 04:07.480
Yes.

04:08.040 --> 04:11.180
And the paper introduces a very specific term here.

04:11.300 --> 04:12.320
They call it compression.

04:12.580 --> 04:13.020
Impression.

04:13.460 --> 04:13.780
Yeah.

04:13.780 --> 04:18.840
Because years ago, the master probably did exactly what the apprentice was about to do.

04:18.840 --> 04:20.580
They tore the whole assembly apart.

04:20.740 --> 04:21.940
They wasted five hours.

04:22.120 --> 04:23.640
They got incredibly frustrated.

04:23.880 --> 04:25.120
Probably threw a wrench across the garage.

04:25.260 --> 04:25.820
Exactly.

04:26.240 --> 04:32.460
And they eventually discovered that hidden wire by sheer accident or just exhausting every other option.

04:33.020 --> 04:40.640
So the master took this sprawling, painful trajectory of searching, and they compressed it into a simple rule.

04:40.640 --> 04:44.740
If you see symptom A, check hidden wire B.

04:44.920 --> 04:49.180
So the apprentice inherited the compression, but they completely skipped the search.

04:49.300 --> 04:49.620
Completely.

04:50.080 --> 04:50.540
It's funny.

04:51.440 --> 04:54.660
Reading this, the very first thing that popped into my head was video games.

04:55.000 --> 04:55.520
Oh, really?

04:55.800 --> 04:56.260
How do you mean?

04:56.740 --> 05:00.420
Well, it's like being handed someone else's save game file.

05:00.520 --> 05:01.460
Oh, I see where you're going.

05:01.880 --> 05:07.660
Like, imagine you load a save file for this massively complex game that you have never personally played.

05:07.660 --> 05:11.320
You boot it up, and your character is max level.

05:11.360 --> 05:11.500
Right.

05:11.560 --> 05:12.620
You've got all the best stuff.

05:12.920 --> 05:13.120
Yeah.

05:13.560 --> 05:15.260
You have the ultimate armor, the best weapons.

05:15.400 --> 05:17.040
You can cast the most powerful spells.

05:17.460 --> 05:23.540
You can walk into the starting zone and just absolutely obliterate the basic enemies with zero effort.

05:23.840 --> 05:25.380
You look incredibly competent.

05:25.660 --> 05:26.080
Exactly.

05:26.260 --> 05:27.100
You look like a pro.

05:27.520 --> 05:32.000
But you have absolutely zero idea how the map is actually laid out.

05:32.000 --> 05:40.240
You don't know, like, the attack patterns of the bosses that the original player had to painstakingly memorize to earn that gear.

05:40.560 --> 05:44.080
That is a phenomenal translation of what the paper calls borrowed intuition.

05:44.500 --> 05:44.740
Yeah.

05:44.960 --> 05:52.680
You obtain this economical disposition, the ability to act swiftly and correctly, but without incurring the cost of its discovery.

05:52.980 --> 05:53.220
Right.

05:53.400 --> 05:59.940
But there's a nuance here that we really need to clarify, because we normally think of learning from someone else as just, you know, memorizing facts.

06:00.000 --> 06:01.200
Like reading a textbook in school.

06:01.200 --> 06:01.720
Exactly.

06:02.080 --> 06:05.160
In philosophy, that's called propositional testimony.

06:05.480 --> 06:05.920
Propositional.

06:06.080 --> 06:06.300
Right.

06:06.780 --> 06:08.960
That's me telling you the capital of France is Paris.

06:09.220 --> 06:11.880
I give you a proposition and you just accept it as true.

06:12.360 --> 06:15.900
But what the master mechanic gave the apprentice is much deeper than that.

06:16.020 --> 06:17.540
It's procedural testimony.

06:18.120 --> 06:18.600
Procedural.

06:18.860 --> 06:19.860
So it's not just a fact.

06:19.920 --> 06:20.840
It's an actual method.

06:20.980 --> 06:23.060
It is a transmitted policy for inquiry.

06:23.180 --> 06:23.640
Oh, wow.

06:23.760 --> 06:23.960
Yeah.

06:24.220 --> 06:27.040
The master didn't just say the wire is broken.

06:27.560 --> 06:30.520
The master said, here is how you should structure your search.

06:30.520 --> 06:32.380
Ignore the obvious symptom.

06:33.280 --> 06:34.860
Prioritize this hidden area.

06:35.380 --> 06:37.240
Here is where you can safely stop looking.

06:37.760 --> 06:42.100
It basically reorganizes the apprentice's entire approach to the physical world.

06:42.180 --> 06:42.640
Exactly.

06:42.640 --> 06:46.180
And the scary part is how invisible that feels.

06:46.500 --> 06:51.020
Like when the apprentice checks the wire six months later, it feels like instinct.

06:51.180 --> 06:52.380
It feels like it's their intuition.

06:52.600 --> 06:53.000
Yes.

06:53.260 --> 06:58.800
But the historical provenance of that skill is entirely borrowed.

06:59.020 --> 07:04.100
Which, you know, naturally leads to a bit of an existential crisis if you really follow the logic all the way down.

07:04.100 --> 07:04.460
Oh, yeah.

07:04.760 --> 07:09.380
Because, I mean, if I look at my own life, I didn't invent the alphabet I used to speak.

07:09.500 --> 07:12.480
I didn't derive the principles of calculus I used for data analysis.

07:12.840 --> 07:12.900
Right.

07:13.020 --> 07:16.480
I didn't run the clinical trials for the ibuprofen I take when I have a headache.

07:16.860 --> 07:16.880
You know.

07:16.900 --> 07:17.700
Or just take the pill.

07:18.200 --> 07:18.600
Exactly.

07:18.600 --> 07:29.660
If literally every tool and concept I use is a compressed shortcut handed down by a previous generation, does that mean I'm just this hollow vessel of borrowed intuition?

07:29.820 --> 07:29.960
Yeah.

07:30.080 --> 07:32.340
Like, is all my competence somehow fake?

07:32.640 --> 07:39.840
I am so glad you brought that up because the paper explicitly warns against this kind of epistemic despair.

07:40.020 --> 07:40.560
Oh, good.

07:41.200 --> 07:42.260
Because I was getting worried.

07:42.380 --> 07:42.640
Yeah.

07:42.760 --> 07:44.860
No, borrowed intuition is not fake competence.

07:45.080 --> 07:46.140
It is highly functional.

07:46.140 --> 07:48.800
I mean, the apprentice really did fix the engine.

07:49.380 --> 07:49.440
Yeah.

07:49.540 --> 07:51.940
The ibuprofen really does cure your headache.

07:52.220 --> 07:55.220
The difference isn't about whether the knowledge is real or fake.

07:55.340 --> 07:58.900
It's about mapping out the exact limits of that knowledge.

07:59.000 --> 07:59.200
Okay.

07:59.320 --> 08:04.100
Because if you don't know how you learn something, you won't know what to do when that rule suddenly stops working.

08:04.440 --> 08:04.740
Okay.

08:04.920 --> 08:06.160
So how do we map it out?

08:06.240 --> 08:11.860
Because clearly, like, reading a manual is fundamentally different from burning your hand on a hot engine block.

08:11.940 --> 08:12.340
Absolutely.

08:12.720 --> 08:15.360
But both result in you knowing not to touch it.

08:15.360 --> 08:15.840
Right.

08:16.140 --> 08:20.720
And the paper constructs this really elegant matrix to map this out.

08:20.940 --> 08:27.680
It basically breaks down all of our operative competence into two dimensions, provenance and formation.

08:28.140 --> 08:28.700
All right.

08:28.740 --> 08:30.160
I know this part gets a bit dense.

08:30.260 --> 08:31.320
So let's take our time with you.

08:31.380 --> 08:31.580
Sure.

08:31.700 --> 08:33.100
Let's start with provenance.

08:33.300 --> 08:36.240
What does that actually mean in this context?

08:36.240 --> 08:41.260
So provenance is simply asking, where did the evidential support for this rule come from?

08:41.360 --> 08:43.100
Did it come from you or someone else?

08:43.100 --> 08:43.360
Okay.

08:43.600 --> 08:46.940
On one extreme of the spectrum, you have earned provenance.

08:47.000 --> 08:47.440
Earned.

08:47.480 --> 08:47.680
Right.

08:47.680 --> 08:54.160
This means the rule was stabilized by evidence you generated through your own trial and error.

08:54.400 --> 08:55.300
You burned your hand.

08:55.380 --> 08:56.180
You broke the engine.

08:56.500 --> 08:58.640
You saw the failure with your own eyes.

08:58.740 --> 09:01.780
So back to the game analogy, that's playing the game from level one.

09:01.900 --> 09:02.260
Exactly.

09:02.260 --> 09:04.680
On the other end, you have transmitted provenance.

09:04.780 --> 09:04.940
Okay.

09:04.940 --> 09:11.160
Meaning the search, the failure, and the selection of the correct rule happens somewhere else in someone else's life.

09:11.480 --> 09:14.800
And the finished, compressed shortcut was just handed to you.

09:15.040 --> 09:17.340
So that's provenance, the origin of the evidence.

09:17.480 --> 09:17.780
Yes.

09:17.960 --> 09:18.860
What about the second dimension?

09:19.400 --> 09:19.840
Formation?

09:20.140 --> 09:24.320
So formation looks at the environment where you actually acquired the skill.

09:24.500 --> 09:26.920
Like who was controlling your exposure to the problem.

09:27.040 --> 09:27.260
Okay.

09:27.260 --> 09:29.180
On one side is self-directed formation.

09:30.020 --> 09:31.100
You are in the wild.

09:31.580 --> 09:32.720
You choose what to investigate.

09:33.100 --> 09:35.260
You decide which errors are worth pursuing.

09:35.660 --> 09:36.880
You set your own goals.

09:36.960 --> 09:39.620
Like a scientist designing their own novel experiment.

09:39.680 --> 09:40.300
Perfect example.

09:40.460 --> 09:40.680
Yes.

09:40.680 --> 09:40.780
Yeah.

09:41.100 --> 09:45.040
But most of our education falls on the other side, which is conditioned formation.

09:45.200 --> 09:45.600
Condition.

09:45.860 --> 09:46.060
Yeah.

09:46.060 --> 09:53.900
This is when a teacher or a university, an algorithm, or a corporate training program heavily curates your environment.

09:54.200 --> 09:55.880
They feed you specific problems.

09:56.000 --> 09:56.980
They constrain the variables.

09:57.260 --> 10:00.040
And they provide immediate structured feedback.

10:00.360 --> 10:00.540
Okay.

10:00.580 --> 10:00.740
Wait.

10:01.200 --> 10:03.060
Let me push back on this framework for a second.

10:03.160 --> 10:03.560
Go for it.

10:03.660 --> 10:10.600
Because if I'm a software developer, right, and I'm writing code, I'm using a program language someone else invented.

10:10.760 --> 10:12.120
So that's transmitted provenance.

10:12.180 --> 10:12.340
Right.

10:12.340 --> 10:15.240
And I probably learned it in a highly structured boot camp.

10:15.340 --> 10:16.380
So that's conditioned formation.

10:16.860 --> 10:18.920
Does that mean I have zero earned intuition?

10:19.160 --> 10:24.060
Because, I mean, my hands are literally on the keyboard typing original programs every single day.

10:24.760 --> 10:29.320
I'm solving problems no one has solved before, even if I'm using borrowed tools.

10:29.940 --> 10:32.180
And that is the exact tension the paper anticipates.

10:32.280 --> 10:35.400
If the framework just stopped there, it would be way too rigid.

10:35.560 --> 10:35.700
Yeah.

10:35.740 --> 10:36.680
It feels a bit black and white.

10:36.680 --> 10:42.500
It would imply you are either a pure independent pioneer or a total unthinking clone.

10:43.060 --> 10:45.920
But human learning is far more dynamic than that.

10:46.040 --> 10:52.160
So to explain your software developer example, the paper introduces a concept called synthetic compression.

10:52.760 --> 10:53.980
Synthetic compression.

10:54.200 --> 10:54.920
I like the sound of that.

10:55.020 --> 10:56.160
How does it bridge the gap?

10:56.160 --> 11:08.720
Synthetic compression is what happens when you take a borrowed rule, something with transmitted provenance, and you drag it out into the messy real world and start testing it through your own trajectory.

11:09.100 --> 11:09.320
Right.

11:09.420 --> 11:15.660
You take the master's advice about the hidden wire, but over the next 10 years, you apply it to 500 different cars.

11:15.900 --> 11:16.280
Oh, I see.

11:16.280 --> 11:21.900
So it's like, let's say I inherit my grandmother's famous chocolate chip cookie recipe.

11:22.100 --> 11:22.220
Okay.

11:22.480 --> 11:22.680
Yeah.

11:23.020 --> 11:25.580
The recipe itself is pure transmitted compression.

11:26.820 --> 11:29.720
I didn't figure out the ratio of flour to sugar she did.

11:29.800 --> 11:30.060
Right.

11:30.180 --> 11:31.540
You just have the piece of paper in your hand.

11:31.620 --> 11:31.980
Exactly.

11:32.440 --> 11:36.540
But then I take it to my apartment, and my oven is terrible.

11:36.800 --> 11:38.860
It runs way hotter than hers did.

11:38.980 --> 11:39.320
Mm-hmm.

11:39.560 --> 11:44.140
So I follow the recipe perfectly, and I burn the first batch to an absolute crisp.

11:44.300 --> 11:44.820
Yeah, truly.

11:44.820 --> 11:47.880
Then I adjust the temperature down by 20 degrees.

11:48.260 --> 11:49.180
I bake it again.

11:49.260 --> 11:50.180
They come out a little doughy.

11:50.840 --> 11:54.680
Finally, I figure out the exact bake time for my specific oven.

11:55.360 --> 12:01.620
So I started with her transmitted rule, but I synthesized it with my own earned painful experience of eating burnt cookies.

12:01.780 --> 12:03.140
Which is the worst kind of pain.

12:03.280 --> 12:03.540
Right.

12:03.820 --> 12:04.060
Huh.

12:04.340 --> 12:06.600
And now, it's truly my recipe.

12:06.820 --> 12:08.260
That is a flawless analogy.

12:08.780 --> 12:11.140
You discovered the boundary conditions of the borrowed rule.

12:11.280 --> 12:12.600
You found out where it breaks.

12:12.600 --> 12:14.580
You added your own earned evidence.

12:14.820 --> 12:16.440
To the transmitted bus line.

12:16.780 --> 12:21.840
So a portion of that operative competence is now supported by your own pausal history.

12:22.260 --> 12:23.520
But there's a trap here, isn't there?

12:23.540 --> 12:24.280
Oh, absolutely.

12:24.540 --> 12:27.860
Because I can think of people I've worked with who have been doing a job for 20 years.

12:28.040 --> 12:28.360
Mm-hmm.

12:28.480 --> 12:29.820
But they haven't synthesized anything.

12:30.060 --> 12:30.320
Yeah.

12:30.540 --> 12:33.980
It's like they just repeated their first year on the job 20 times in a row.

12:33.980 --> 12:34.500
Yes.

12:34.920 --> 12:38.900
The paper makes a very sharp, very important distinction here.

12:39.620 --> 12:42.020
Repetition does not equal validation.

12:42.660 --> 12:43.540
Break that down for me.

12:44.260 --> 12:48.580
If I do a task 1,000 times, how is that not earning the experience?

12:48.680 --> 12:50.140
I mean, my hands are doing the work.

12:50.140 --> 12:55.640
Because if you perform a borrowed task 1,000 times in the exact same unchanging environment,

12:56.060 --> 13:01.820
and it works flawlessly every single time, you haven't actually learned anything new about the rule.

13:02.480 --> 13:02.680
Oh?

13:02.840 --> 13:05.980
You haven't earned the compression in a true epistemic sense.

13:06.060 --> 13:07.880
You've just increased your muscle memory.

13:08.020 --> 13:08.320
Ah.

13:08.760 --> 13:10.520
Because I never actually saw it fail.

13:10.520 --> 13:10.960
Exactly.

13:11.500 --> 13:19.140
For synthetic compression to actually take place, your ongoing experience must supply what the paper calls discriminating feedback.

13:19.360 --> 13:20.300
Discriminating feedback.

13:20.580 --> 13:23.900
You have to encounter situations that genuinely test the rule.

13:24.320 --> 13:27.240
You have to bump up against anomalies where the recipe burns.

13:27.700 --> 13:32.000
If your environment merely confirms what the borrowed rule already predicts,

13:32.280 --> 13:38.620
your behavior becomes incredibly stable, sure, but the foundation of your knowledge hasn't deepened one inch.

13:38.620 --> 13:43.300
You still don't know why the rule works or what alternatives might have been better.

13:43.700 --> 13:44.640
So you need friction.

13:45.000 --> 13:45.260
Yeah.

13:45.380 --> 13:47.760
You need failure to actually own the knowledge.

13:47.900 --> 13:49.140
You need separating evidence.

13:49.300 --> 13:51.620
Without it, you are just a very fluent mimic.

13:51.900 --> 13:52.840
That is fascinating.

13:52.960 --> 13:59.260
So an individual expert is basically walking around with this incredibly complex, invisible tapestry in their head.

13:59.960 --> 14:05.700
Some threads are deeply earned from their own burnt cookies, and some threads are purely borrowed from their mentors.

14:05.700 --> 14:10.580
But here is a question that really got me thinking while prepping for this.

14:10.720 --> 14:11.160
What's that?

14:11.760 --> 14:13.820
What happens when those experts retire?

14:14.500 --> 14:22.440
Like, how does a whole society hold onto these shortcuts when the actual people who originally did the hard, painful search are gone?

14:22.440 --> 14:26.060
And that is exactly where we scale up from the individual to the institution.

14:26.300 --> 14:26.560
Okay.

14:26.560 --> 14:31.960
Because if one human brain can compress a five-hour search into a five-second rule of thumb,

14:32.540 --> 14:40.400
an institution is basically a machine designed to compress thousands of searches across decades into durable physical artifacts.

14:40.400 --> 14:46.600
So we're talking about things like corporate policy manuals, safety regulations, standard operating procedures.

14:46.940 --> 14:47.540
Exactly.

14:48.240 --> 14:53.060
The paper looks at institutional memory not just as, like, a dusty filing cabinet,

14:53.380 --> 15:00.180
but as a social technology for transmitting compressed policies across what it calls discontinuities of personnel.

15:00.420 --> 15:01.920
Meaning people quit, people die.

15:01.980 --> 15:02.180
Right.

15:02.260 --> 15:05.680
But the institution ensures the shortcut survives them.

15:06.160 --> 15:07.880
You know what always strikes me about this?

15:08.340 --> 15:09.600
Aviation checklists.

15:09.600 --> 15:11.780
Oh, the ultimate example.

15:11.940 --> 15:20.140
I was reading a book on this a while ago, and I realized that a pre-flight safety checklist is essentially a ghost story written by past crashes.

15:20.300 --> 15:21.080
A ghost story.

15:21.600 --> 15:25.000
That is morbid, but perfectly aligned with the paper's framework.

15:25.280 --> 15:25.920
Think about it.

15:26.100 --> 15:32.340
You look at one of those laminated cards in the cockpit, and there's always some hyper-specific, weirdly mundane instruction.

15:32.640 --> 15:35.100
Yeah, like flip this one obscure switch.

15:35.100 --> 15:42.880
Right, like ensure the left auxiliary pitot tube heater is toggled on for exactly three minutes before taxiing and freezing rain.

15:43.000 --> 15:43.260
Right.

15:43.560 --> 15:47.060
A rule that specific didn't just come from a theoretical brainstorm in a boardroom.

15:47.300 --> 15:55.420
It came because 20 years ago, someone didn't turn that heater on, the sensor froze, the airspeed readings went crazy, and a plane went down.

15:55.420 --> 15:58.040
And the search after that crash was agonizing.

15:58.640 --> 16:04.820
It involved wreckage, the NTSB, competing aerodynamic models, and probably years of debate.

16:04.960 --> 16:05.200
Right.

16:05.440 --> 16:11.500
And all of that trauma, all of that science, gets compressed into one single bullet point on a plastic card.

16:11.500 --> 16:12.060
Mm-hmm.

16:12.400 --> 16:16.140
The pilots flying today follow that step religiously.

16:16.720 --> 16:19.440
They don't need to experience the crash to get the lesson.

16:19.820 --> 16:26.560
The institution is basically acting as the medium, passing the ghost's warning directly into the pilot's hands.

16:26.680 --> 16:29.600
Which is the ultimate triumph of human civilization, really.

16:29.720 --> 16:31.920
We don't have to reinvent the airplane every generation.

16:32.040 --> 16:32.320
Thankfully.

16:32.320 --> 16:32.720
Yeah.

16:33.480 --> 16:42.780
The current pilot gets the immense practical safety benefit of the investigation without having to know the names of the investigators or what other theories they tested and discarded.

16:42.980 --> 16:44.160
It's incredibly efficient.

16:44.440 --> 16:44.600
Yeah.

16:44.740 --> 16:47.440
But the paper points out a massive vulnerability here, right?

16:47.460 --> 16:47.580
Yeah.

16:47.620 --> 16:50.740
Like, what happens when the ghost story gets separated from the rule?

16:51.080 --> 16:53.900
The paper calls this the risk of provenance loss.

16:54.060 --> 16:54.940
Provenance loss.

16:54.940 --> 16:55.580
Yes.

16:56.100 --> 17:07.100
As a policy moves from the original messy investigation to a formalized technical report to a regulatory standard and finally ends up as just a simple laminated checklist.

17:07.420 --> 17:07.600
Yeah.

17:07.780 --> 17:13.980
The connection between what you should do and why you should do it becomes dangerously thin.

17:14.240 --> 17:17.700
And the paper uses some specific notation for this, doesn't it?

17:17.920 --> 17:19.260
Thin versus thick records.

17:19.420 --> 17:19.880
It does.

17:19.880 --> 17:24.760
To understand provenance loss, we have to look at how the institution stores the information.

17:25.620 --> 17:30.420
A thin record, which the paper denotes as R-thin, is just the current operative instruction.

17:30.960 --> 17:32.200
It's the laminated card.

17:32.440 --> 17:33.140
Toggle the heater.

17:33.560 --> 17:36.360
It is highly optimized for fast, frictionless transmission.

17:36.820 --> 17:37.220
Right.

17:37.280 --> 17:38.400
Which is what you want in an emergency.

17:38.540 --> 17:40.540
You don't want to read a textbook while the plane is moving.

17:40.660 --> 17:41.120
Precisely.

17:41.420 --> 17:44.160
But a thick record R-thick is the actual archive.

17:44.480 --> 17:48.600
It includes the instruction, but it also includes the originating conditions of the crash,

17:48.600 --> 17:55.140
the weather data, the alternative hypotheses that were rejected, the history of how the rule has been revised over 20 years,

17:55.220 --> 17:59.220
and critically, the known edge cases where the rule might actually be counterproductive.

17:59.220 --> 18:05.480
So the thick record is like the full NTSB crash report sitting in a basement somewhere in Washington, D.C.

18:05.480 --> 18:06.120
Exactly.

18:06.840 --> 18:13.820
Now, imagine a scenario where an institution relies entirely on thin records for its daily operations,

18:14.120 --> 18:16.280
and suddenly the environment changes.

18:16.580 --> 18:17.320
Let's play this out.

18:17.460 --> 18:25.740
So, say the airline buys a brand new fleet of planes, and on this new model, the engineers completely redesigned the sensor system.

18:25.820 --> 18:26.160
All right.

18:26.860 --> 18:29.180
The pitot tubes don't even use external heaters anymore.

18:29.420 --> 18:32.820
They use some new internal thermal system that just runs automatically.

18:32.820 --> 18:37.060
But the bureaucracy hasn't updated the laminated checklist.

18:37.540 --> 18:38.480
So what does the pilot do?

18:39.060 --> 18:45.020
The pilot sits in the freezing rain, reads the thin record, and spends three minutes hunting for a toggle switch

18:45.020 --> 18:49.020
that either doesn't exist or, worse, is now wired to something completely different.

18:49.100 --> 18:49.300
Right.

18:49.500 --> 18:54.020
They're executing the rule flawlessly, but the rule is entirely divorced from reality.

18:54.240 --> 18:57.080
And that is the exact danger of provenance loss.

18:57.520 --> 19:00.380
The action has basically devolved into a blind ritual.

19:00.380 --> 19:08.740
The paper states that a policy can remain institutionally stable long after the underlying reality that justified it has vanished.

19:08.840 --> 19:09.280
Oh, wow.

19:09.420 --> 19:09.580
Yeah.

19:09.940 --> 19:18.420
Without access to the thick record, the current operators have no practical way to distinguish a vital safety protocol from a dangerous, obsolete habit.

19:18.620 --> 19:20.440
Because they only have the borrowed intuition.

19:20.440 --> 19:27.740
They don't have the original earned experience to tell them why the rule existed, so they don't have the intuition to know when it's safe to break it.

19:27.860 --> 19:32.440
This is why healthy institutions go to such immense lengths to maintain those thick records.

19:32.920 --> 19:37.960
Incident reports, revision histories, even memos documenting internal disagreements.

19:38.080 --> 19:38.220
Right.

19:38.540 --> 19:45.260
When a procedure fails in the field, the operators need to be able to pull up the thick record and reconstruct the original problem space.

19:45.260 --> 19:49.860
They have to unpack the compression to see if the foundational assumptions still hold true.

19:50.300 --> 19:54.360
If the thick record is lost, the institution is flying entirely blind.

19:54.700 --> 19:57.020
That thought is deeply unsettling.

19:57.640 --> 19:59.960
We have these massive global systems, right?

20:00.180 --> 20:02.240
Healthcare, aviation, finance.

20:02.660 --> 20:03.940
Running on autopilot.

20:04.440 --> 20:07.960
Executing rules that might be totally disconnected from the current environment.

20:08.100 --> 20:09.740
It happens more than we'd like to admit.

20:10.080 --> 20:13.600
But as I was reading this section, a huge light bulb went off for me.

20:13.600 --> 20:21.960
If an institution is basically just a social technology for distributing compressed intuition without the original experience,

20:22.200 --> 20:24.800
then modern computing hasn't just copied this.

20:25.040 --> 20:29.100
It has built the ultimate automated super-scaled version of an institution.

20:29.520 --> 20:30.280
You're talking about AI.

20:30.700 --> 20:31.160
I am.

20:31.460 --> 20:34.080
This was the most mind-bending part of the paper for me.

20:34.280 --> 20:39.860
It completely reframes the entire cultural argument we are having right now about artificial intelligence.

20:40.120 --> 20:42.720
I'm curious, how did it shift your perspective on that?

20:42.720 --> 20:45.380
Well, think about the debates we see every day, right?

20:45.480 --> 20:50.080
People arguing on podcasts or in articles about whether a large language model is conscious,

20:50.280 --> 20:55.900
or if it truly understands the meaning of the words it generates, or if it possesses a genuine world model.

20:56.020 --> 20:56.220
Right.

20:56.300 --> 20:58.040
The classic philosophical debates.

20:58.440 --> 20:58.840
Exactly.

20:59.280 --> 21:03.360
But the Flyxion paper steps back from all that philosophical noise and basically says,

21:03.460 --> 21:05.860
you don't need to answer those metaphysical questions.

21:05.920 --> 21:09.720
You just need to look at the causal organization of its learning pipeline.

21:09.720 --> 21:10.440
Yes.

21:11.080 --> 21:18.560
The paper views the pre-trained language model as the absolute extreme limiting case of borrowed intuition.

21:19.040 --> 21:21.480
It is the ultimate thin record.

21:21.740 --> 21:22.120
Truly.

21:22.400 --> 21:23.020
Think about it.

21:23.260 --> 21:26.160
An LLM ingests a massive corpus of human text.

21:26.640 --> 21:29.780
That text is a graveyard of human trial and error.

21:30.220 --> 21:36.060
It's centuries of scientific experiments, philosophical arguments, medical clinical trials, coding failures.

21:36.060 --> 21:42.040
All of that earned, agonizing human search is scraped up, and the AI optimizes over it.

21:42.320 --> 21:46.480
So you can ask a medical AI for a diagnosis, and it'll give you an answer in two seconds.

21:46.540 --> 21:51.020
It took human doctors centuries of blood, sweat, and autopsies to actually figure out.

21:51.100 --> 21:51.300
Right.

21:51.560 --> 21:55.280
It has absolute terrifying fluency, but it has zero autobiography.

21:55.420 --> 21:57.720
That is the crucial distinction the paper makes.

21:57.880 --> 22:00.900
The machine experienced none of the causal history of the inquiry.

22:01.060 --> 22:01.840
It didn't hold a scalpel.

22:01.940 --> 22:03.920
It didn't watch a patient's reaction to a drug.

22:03.920 --> 22:06.700
The paper is incredibly strict on this point.

22:07.460 --> 22:13.920
Optimizing statistical weights over a corpus of text is a trajectory of adjusting mathematical parameters, yes.

22:14.500 --> 22:20.520
But it does not place the system within the worldly causal history of the original human inquiry.

22:21.060 --> 22:22.860
So just reading about it isn't doing it.

22:22.940 --> 22:23.340
Exactly.

22:23.920 --> 22:27.200
A record of an experiment is not the experiment itself.

22:27.440 --> 22:29.160
It's parameter transmitted compression.

22:29.160 --> 22:40.300
The AI is inheriting the statistical residue of millions of human struggles, and it's compressed into a single executable file.

22:40.960 --> 22:44.260
It's the video game save file on a planetary scale.

22:44.380 --> 22:45.500
That's exactly what it is.

22:45.600 --> 22:49.660
The machine lacks any autobiographical relation to the knowledge it's deploying.

22:49.960 --> 22:52.440
But wait, let me channel the AI defenders for a second.

22:52.480 --> 22:53.180
Okay, let's hear it.

22:53.180 --> 23:01.040
Because someone listening is screaming right now, but I chat with the AI, I give it a prompt, it makes a mistake, I correct it, and it apologizes and changes its answer.

23:01.260 --> 23:02.140
It adapts to me.

23:02.340 --> 23:03.100
Isn't that learning?

23:03.320 --> 23:03.560
Right.

23:03.780 --> 23:06.940
Isn't it generating its own earned compression right there in the chat?

23:06.940 --> 23:16.400
It's a really great question, and the paper breaks down the different phases of AI pipelines specifically to address this confusion, because people conflate different mechanisms.

23:16.500 --> 23:17.280
Okay, so break it down.

23:17.500 --> 23:18.560
Let's look at what you just described.

23:18.920 --> 23:21.900
Giving the AI a prompt and correcting it in real time.

23:22.400 --> 23:25.580
In computer science, this is called in-context learning.

23:25.800 --> 23:26.840
Right, in-context.

23:27.000 --> 23:31.180
The paper argues that mechanically, in-context learning is merely temporary conditioning.

23:31.380 --> 23:31.900
Temporary?

23:31.900 --> 23:44.100
Yes, the model adapts its response within the active context window of your chat, but the underlying neural weights, the actual parameters that define the core model, they are frozen.

23:44.540 --> 23:45.640
They don't change at all.

23:45.960 --> 23:56.300
So it's like having a really intense conversation with someone that temporarily changes their mood for an hour, but it doesn't actually alter their long-term memory or their underlying personality.

23:56.580 --> 23:56.760
Yeah.

23:57.120 --> 23:59.520
Once they go to sleep and wake up, the conversation is gone.

23:59.640 --> 24:00.660
That is exactly it.

24:00.660 --> 24:03.540
Once you clear the chat window, the adaptation vanishes.

24:04.040 --> 24:07.920
It doesn't reorganize the system's fundamental search policy for the next user.

24:08.460 --> 24:11.260
Therefore, it is not stabilized earned compression.

24:11.660 --> 24:12.440
Okay, I get that.

24:12.520 --> 24:13.800
The context window is temporary.

24:13.800 --> 24:14.520
Mm-hmm.

24:14.520 --> 24:18.980
But what about the training they do after the initial pre-training, like RLHF?

24:19.380 --> 24:21.200
Reinforcement learning from human feedback.

24:21.680 --> 24:22.200
Exactly.

24:22.520 --> 24:26.160
That actually does change the model's underlying behavior permanently, right?

24:26.360 --> 24:28.100
It makes it safer or more helpful.

24:28.420 --> 24:30.160
It does change the weights, yes.

24:30.660 --> 24:35.540
But we have to ask, from a provenance perspective, what is actually happening during RLHF?

24:35.740 --> 24:36.020
Okay.

24:36.940 --> 24:46.540
Mechanically, humans sit in a room, they give the model prompts, the model generates two answers, and the human ranks which one is better based on a set of corporate guidelines.

24:46.540 --> 24:46.860
Right.

24:47.020 --> 24:50.760
They're training it to be polite and helpful, not to swear, that kind of thing.

24:50.760 --> 24:52.820
But look at the causal structure.

24:53.600 --> 24:57.300
The evaluative signal is entirely externally authored by humans.

24:58.080 --> 25:03.020
The model isn't out in the physical world testing a hypothesis and observing a natural consequence.

25:03.380 --> 25:03.700
Oh, I see.

25:03.700 --> 25:06.400
It is being heavily conditioned by human constraints.

25:06.920 --> 25:10.100
The paper categorizes RLHF as conditioned formation.

25:10.660 --> 25:15.500
The model is simply receiving more transmitted compression, this time about human preferences.

25:16.020 --> 25:16.680
Oh, wow.

25:16.880 --> 25:26.360
So even when we think we are teaching the AI to think for itself, we are really just force-feeding it our own borrowed intuition about what a quote-unquote good answer looks like.

25:26.440 --> 25:26.920
Yes.

25:26.920 --> 25:29.180
It is entirely dependent on earthic records.

25:29.280 --> 25:29.840
Exactly.

25:30.320 --> 25:32.400
It isn't self-directing and inquiry.

25:33.160 --> 25:40.580
For an AI to achieve true synthetic compression, where it actually earns its own evidential support the way a human master mechanic does,

25:40.980 --> 25:48.060
the paper points toward architectures involving continual learning or persistent long-term memory situated in dynamic environments.

25:48.380 --> 25:51.840
So what would that actually look like mechanically, if not a chatbot?

25:51.840 --> 26:01.160
Well, the system would have to take an action in a complex environment, say a robotic system manipulating physical objects, or an AI managing a live power grid.

26:01.220 --> 26:01.940
Okay, real things.

26:02.200 --> 26:02.480
Right.

26:02.700 --> 26:06.260
It would have to observe the discriminating feedback from reality itself.

26:06.780 --> 26:15.060
When the power grid fails, the AI must use that failure to persistently update its own core parameters, which then permanently changes its future policy.

26:15.420 --> 26:19.900
And critically, it would need autonomy to choose what to explore and how to weigh the errors.

26:19.900 --> 26:29.060
So until an AI is acting in the world with real stakes, making its own unique mistakes, and rewriting its core architecture based on the friction of reality,

26:29.340 --> 26:35.160
it is essentially just a gigantic frozen repository of human borrowed intuition.

26:35.540 --> 26:39.260
It is an extreme automated case of a very familiar arrangement.

26:39.740 --> 26:41.420
It's not some alien epistemology.

26:41.760 --> 26:47.260
It is the institutional transmission mechanism pushed to its absolute computational limit.

26:47.260 --> 26:52.680
Which brings us to the most practical and, frankly, the most high-stakes question of this entire deep dive.

26:52.820 --> 26:53.640
Let's get into it.

26:53.700 --> 26:58.280
Whether we're talking about a junior doctor relying on a hospital software algorithm they didn't write,

26:58.480 --> 27:05.040
or a software developer using an AI to generate code based on millions of repositories they haven't read.

27:05.120 --> 27:05.340
Right.

27:05.480 --> 27:09.320
Both are wielding immense power based entirely on borrowed intuition.

27:09.320 --> 27:14.600
So, when things go wrong, and they invariably do, how do we evaluate the failure?

27:14.740 --> 27:18.640
Like, how do we know who is actually competent and who just has a really good save game file?

27:18.760 --> 27:20.920
This is the climax of the paper's argument, really.

27:21.080 --> 27:24.660
It introduces a vital diagnostic concept called the expansion deficit.

27:25.080 --> 27:26.420
The expansion deficit.

27:26.660 --> 27:27.600
This is the core takeaway.

27:28.000 --> 27:28.980
Explain how this works.

27:28.980 --> 27:34.200
So, an expansion deficit is the hidden gap between knowing the compressed rule

27:34.200 --> 27:40.980
and possessing the deeper foundational distinction structures required to fix the rule when it inevitably breaks.

27:41.140 --> 27:42.500
I need a concrete example for this.

27:42.620 --> 27:42.720
Yeah.

27:42.820 --> 27:44.320
Let's go back to the mechanic's garage.

27:44.440 --> 27:44.680
Perfect.

27:45.140 --> 27:46.140
Let's take two agents.

27:46.640 --> 27:48.260
Agent A is our master mechanic.

27:48.960 --> 27:50.840
Their knowledge has earned provenance.

27:51.040 --> 27:51.240
Yeah.

27:51.520 --> 27:52.980
Agent B is the apprentice.

27:53.660 --> 27:54.660
Their knowledge is transmitted.

27:54.660 --> 28:02.500
In a normal, invariant environment, so the everyday routine of the shop, both agents perform flawlessly.

28:03.060 --> 28:04.480
Their outputs look identical.

28:04.800 --> 28:05.000
Right.

28:05.200 --> 28:06.220
A car rolls in.

28:06.380 --> 28:08.000
They both reach behind the firewall.

28:08.200 --> 28:09.520
They both check the hidden wire.

28:09.900 --> 28:11.900
They both fix the car in five minutes.

28:12.100 --> 28:17.420
So, in a stable environment, borrowed intuition is completely indistinguishable from earned intuition.

28:17.540 --> 28:17.880
Exactly.

28:18.160 --> 28:19.600
But now, we introduce an anomaly.

28:20.260 --> 28:22.360
The manufacturer releases a mid-year update.

28:22.360 --> 28:29.180
They completely redesign the internal electrical architecture of the car, but they leave the outer casing exactly the same.

28:29.440 --> 28:29.460
Ah.

28:29.660 --> 28:30.740
So, the environment is shifted?

28:30.860 --> 28:31.080
Yes.

28:31.220 --> 28:31.420
Okay.

28:31.500 --> 28:32.820
So, the car rolls into the shop.

28:33.120 --> 28:33.720
What happens?

28:34.260 --> 28:36.180
Both agents look at the symptoms.

28:36.700 --> 28:41.540
They both apply the compressed rule, reach behind the firewall, and check the wire.

28:41.840 --> 28:46.700
But the wire isn't there, or it's there, but it's now connected to the climate control, not the engine.

28:46.860 --> 28:47.360
Oh, boy.

28:47.480 --> 28:49.160
The rule has completely broken down.

28:49.280 --> 28:51.080
So, what happens to Agent A, the master?

28:51.080 --> 28:55.100
Agent A relies on their deep-earned provenance.

28:55.640 --> 28:58.720
They possess what the paper calls expansion capacity.

28:58.940 --> 28:59.880
Expansion capacity.

29:00.020 --> 29:00.400
Yes.

29:00.920 --> 29:05.520
They realize the rule has failed, and they can mentally uncompress the situation.

29:06.180 --> 29:13.980
They fall back on their foundational understanding of Ohm's law, circuitry, and the causal models of how automotive components interact.

29:14.160 --> 29:15.540
They actually understand the physics.

29:15.540 --> 29:23.280
Right. They can reconstruct the entire problem space from scratch and initiate a completely new, effective search to map out the new design.

29:23.280 --> 29:25.480
They can climb back down the mountain and find a new path up.

29:25.560 --> 29:26.900
What about Agent B, the apprentice?

29:27.420 --> 29:29.060
Agent B is suddenly paralyzed.

29:29.780 --> 29:31.220
Their knowledge stops at the rule.

29:31.460 --> 29:39.340
Because they never underwent the generating trajectory of search, they lack the distinction structures, the deeper why needed to diagnose the failure.

29:39.520 --> 29:39.720
Right.

29:39.720 --> 29:43.880
They just know the trick didn't work, so their performance drops off a cliff.

29:44.140 --> 29:47.680
That catastrophic drop-off is the expansion deficit exposed.

29:48.240 --> 29:49.880
So, what does this mean for us?

29:49.940 --> 29:59.380
If I'm a hospital administrator evaluating doctors or an engineering manager reviewing coders using AI, how do I know who I can trust?

29:59.920 --> 30:04.660
Because in the normal, everyday environment, the apprentice and the master look exactly the same.

30:04.660 --> 30:09.560
And this is where the paper argues that we have to shift our paradigm to provenance-sensitive trust.

30:09.700 --> 30:10.940
Provenance-sensitive trust.

30:11.120 --> 30:13.440
Trust cannot just be based on routine accuracy.

30:13.920 --> 30:16.460
Routine accuracy hides the expansion deficit.

30:16.780 --> 30:18.320
So, what should trust be based on?

30:18.460 --> 30:23.780
Trust must evaluate the match between the generating environment of the rule and the current environment of its use.

30:23.780 --> 30:32.200
If you are operating in a highly stable, completely predictable environment, you can have high operational trust in a borrowed policy.

30:32.640 --> 30:33.720
The checklist will work.

30:34.080 --> 30:36.580
The AI will output good boilerplate code.

30:37.380 --> 30:39.020
But if things get weird...

30:39.020 --> 30:47.120
If you are at the boundary of known conditions or facing a high-stakes anomaly, borrowed intuition becomes incredibly dangerous.

30:47.820 --> 30:52.320
In those moments, you shouldn't just be asking, does this agent know the correct procedure?

30:52.500 --> 30:53.320
You have to ask.

30:53.320 --> 30:58.580
You have to ask, does this agent have the capacity to recognize that the procedure no longer applies?

30:58.900 --> 31:02.260
And if so, can they recover the underlying problem space?

31:02.560 --> 31:05.980
This completely upends how we think about responsibility and blame.

31:06.240 --> 31:07.020
It really does.

31:07.020 --> 31:09.420
Think about how our legal and corporate systems work.

31:10.000 --> 31:16.580
When a machine breaks, or an algorithm denies someone alone unfairly, or a patient gets the wrong dose of medication,

31:17.060 --> 31:22.860
the instinct is always to point the finger at the final operator, the person sitting in the chair who pressed the button.

31:22.860 --> 31:25.000
And the paper is uncompromising on this.

31:25.200 --> 31:32.080
You cannot solely blame the final operator if the institution systematically controlled the constraints and hid the original evidence.

31:32.360 --> 31:34.540
Wait, really? Let's trace the causality on that.

31:35.360 --> 31:40.380
Say a junior doctor is following a mandated hospital algorithm for triaging patients.

31:40.800 --> 31:41.060
Okay.

31:41.060 --> 31:47.300
The algorithm is based on old data, it fails to account for a new viral strain, and a patient suffers.

31:48.560 --> 31:51.940
You're saying we shouldn't just fire the doctor for making a bad call.

31:52.340 --> 31:55.620
Responsibility is distributed across the entire causal chain.

31:55.620 --> 32:03.240
If the hospital administration only provided a thin record, just a mandate saying follow the screen prompts,

32:03.680 --> 32:09.940
with no context and no thick record explaining the data the algorithm was trained on or its known failure states,

32:10.440 --> 32:16.660
then the institution is directly responsible for constructing a dangerously limited distinction space for that doctor.

